
<section class="rounded-[30px] border-2 border-orange-100
                bg-gradient-to-br from-orange-50/60 via-white to-slate-50
                p-6 shadow-sm sm:p-8">

  <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
    <div class="flex h-12 w-12 shrink-0 items-center justify-center
                rounded-2xl border border-orange-200 bg-orange-100 text-2xl">
      💡
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-slate-800">
        Research workspace: administrator reference
      </h2>

      <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
        A workspace groups the information collected during an investigation.
        The purpose is to keep the research question, supporting material,
        observations, and workflow decisions together so the work can be
        reviewed later.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-7 space-y-4">

    <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-orange-100 bg-white p-5">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none text-lg font-black text-slate-800">
        <span class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4">
          What belongs in a research item?
          <span class="text-orange-600 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">⌄</span>
        </span>
      </summary>

      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
        Research items can represent entities, questions, notes, sources, or
        other supported work units. Give each item a descriptive title and
        enough context that another administrator can understand why it exists.
        Keep factual observations separate from assumptions and conclusions.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-orange-100 bg-white p-5">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none text-lg font-black text-slate-800">
        <span class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4">
          How should evidence be evaluated?
          <span class="text-orange-600 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">⌄</span>
        </span>
      </summary>

      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
        Record the original source, its publication date when available,
        the date it was accessed, and the claim it supports. Check whether
        the source directly supports that claim. Seek independent corroboration
        for important conclusions, and document contradictory evidence rather
        than silently discarding it.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-orange-100 bg-white p-5">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none text-lg font-black text-slate-800">
        <span class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4">
          What does a snapshot preserve?
          <span class="text-orange-600 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">⌄</span>
        </span>
      </summary>

      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
        A snapshot is a recorded representation of a workspace at a particular
        point in time. Snapshot restoration can replace current research state,
        depending on the application's restore service. Review the snapshot and
        confirm the intended workspace before restoring it.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-orange-100 bg-white p-5">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none text-lg font-black text-slate-800">
        <span class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4">
          How should completed investigations be handled?
          <span class="text-orange-600 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">⌄</span>
        </span>
      </summary>

      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-slate-600">
        Completion is a workflow decision, not a guarantee that every finding
        is correct. Document unresolved questions and limitations. If new
        evidence changes the conclusion, reopen the investigation using its
        supported lifecycle action and record the reason for further work.
      </p>
    </details>

  </div>

  <div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-amber-200 bg-amber-50 p-5">
    <h3 class="text-lg font-black text-amber-900">
      Important operational rule
    </h3>

    <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-7 text-amber-900">
      Prefer the application's existing lifecycle methods and snapshot
      services over direct database updates. They may record timestamps,
      lifecycle events, and snapshots in addition to changing status.
    </p>
  </div>

</section>